# Findings in git history that are known, judged, and left in place (ADR-0202).
#
# An entry here suppresses one finding at one commit, by fingerprint. It is not a
# path allowlist: a new secret in the same file still fails. Adding a line without
# the reason and the rotation decision beside it defeats the point of the scan.
#
# The remediation for a committed secret is ROTATION, not deletion — reverting the
# file leaves the value in the history that every clone already has. So each entry
# below records what happened to the credential, never merely that it is old.

# TYK_GW_SECRET in a docker-compose.yml, June 2024.
# Rotation is moot: Tyk was the API gateway before Traefik and Oathkeeper, and both
# the component and the file are gone from the tree. There is no gateway holding
# this value and no environment it opens. Left in history rather than rewritten —
# purging it would rewrite every commit since, which is a far larger action than the
# risk warrants for a credential to a system that no longer exists.
